<h1>Welcome to the Out to C docs!</h1>
<p>These are a work in progress but meant to provide some help getting started with your project!</p>
<p>First you'll need to decide which language you want to use for your project. For this event, C, C++ and C# are permitted. All these languages share a common ancestry but are quite different from each other.</p>
<p>C# is often regarded as the easiest of the three, as it is higher level and doesn't require you to interact with concepts such as pointers.</p>
<br>

<details>
<summary>What's the difference between C, C++ and C# ?</summary>
<div>
<p>Quoting <a href="https://www.reddit.com/r/learnprogramming/comments/17auye7/comment/k5f9wni">this</a> reddit reply:</p>
<q>C is mostly used for embedded and systems stuff. It has the advantage of being a pretty small language that's easy to get a handle on - it has the disadvantage of having to do a lot of stuff manually and not having a lot of modern features.

C++ has more OO [Object Oriented] style features, and is heavily used in games and high performance applications. It is a complex language that has a lot of features.

C# is a higher level language that's used in everything from web apps to games and client software. It's one of my personal favorite languages, and is widely usable. It has OO and other features, and is probably the easiest of the three to learn (there's more to learn than C, but it's less likely for you to shoot yourself in the foot). </q>
</div>
</details>

